# Hebrew range "to" boundary that is licensed ONLY after an explicit "from"
# lead (מ־/מן) or a "between" lead (בין) -- the proclitic ל־ ("to/for").
#
# ל־ is the everyday directional/dative preposition ("נסעתי לתל אביב" -- "I
# travelled to Tel Aviv"; "נתתי לו" -- "I gave to him"), a homograph that is
# FAR more common as a plain preposition than as a range terminal.  Registered
# globally as a range "to" marker it would fabricate a range out of any bare
# "ל־<noun>", so it is kept out of the unconditional ``marker_to`` set (which
# holds only עד, the unambiguous "from...until..." terminal) and gated here:
# the engine wires these surfaces as range terminators only when a from-lead
# (מ־/מן) or a between-lead (בין) sits earlier in the span, exactly the
# lead-required guard the Romance directional "a" uses ("de junio a agosto"
# fires; a bare "junio a las tres" does not).  So "מ־ינואר ל־אפריל" /
# "בין ינואר ל־אפריל" resolve as ranges, while a bare "ל־3 שעות" ("for 3
# hours") is left untouched.
#
# The proclitic attaches to its noun in running text ("לאפריל"); set off by a
# maqaf/hyphen ("ל-אפריל") the tokenizer leaves ל as its own token, which is
# the surface matched here.  The fused form is deliberately NOT split.
# "ל־" (le-/la-): L. Glinert, *The Grammar of Modern Hebrew* (Cambridge UP),
#   §9.2, §12 -- the inseparable preposition ל־ "to, for"; in the alternative
#   temporal-range framing "מ... ל..." it is the terminal correlative of מ־
#   "from" (Glinert notes "בין ... ל..." as the equally standard between-form
#   alongside "בין ... ו...").
# Also: Academy of the Hebrew Language, s.v. ל־.
ל
